  "worked_solution": "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14247841/assets/89c82b8d1f3eaf79c7df0a67f377a240a949dda328cd2f448370077d2871c244.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & ={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{31}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{31}) \\\\  & \\,+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})+{M}_{32}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{32}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $6$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{31}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}+{M}_{32}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+2y-3$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+2y-3$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14247841/assets/7888eaa17b26267cc8bed166500ba504fe5a893b30eb90876f2074520e28e8ba.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14247841/assets/eadef808ebc8ff5503de8cefbbbf3a1d64bf3149f0c0c80166c891550ddee91d.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(2,3)=5 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(3,3)=6 \\\\ {M}_{31} & =f(4,3)=7 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(2,4)=7 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(3,4)=8 \\\\ {M}_{32} & =f(4,4)=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =5+6+7+7+8+9 \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
